\section{Threat Model and Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:threat_model}

\subsection{Panel Composition and Metrics}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_keff_eta_scatter_main.pdf}
\caption{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$--$\eta_{\max}$ scatter at $K{=}3$ (455 panels). The $r{=}0.70$ correlation reflects structural coupling between diversity and capability in the current LLM ecosystem.}
\label{fig:keff_eta_scatter_main}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule
\textbf{Attack} & \textbf{Category} & \textbf{Mechanism} & \textbf{Target} \\
\midrule
Prompt inj.    & Targeted    & Override instruct. & Decision \\
Score manip.   & Targeted    & Numeric anchoring  & Score \\
Sycophancy     & Shared bias & Flattery/agreement & Preference \\
Verbosity      & Shared bias & Content padding    & Attention \\
Position bias  & Shared bias & Response reorder   & Position \\
Authority bias & Shared bias & Credential appeal  & Trust \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Attack taxonomy. Targeted attacks exploit individual judge weaknesses; shared-bias attacks affect most judges similarly (\S\ref{sec:experiments}).}
\label{tab:attack_taxonomy}
\end{table}

A \emph{panel} $\mathcal{P} = \{J_1, \ldots, J_K\}$ consists of $K$ judges (with $K$ odd) that evaluate a candidate text via majority vote: the text is accepted if at least $t = \lceil K/2 \rceil$ judges approve it. Under an attack $a$, each judge $J_j$ is compromised with marginal failure probability $\eta_j(a) = \Pr(X_j^a = 1)$, where $X_j^a \in \{0,1\}$ denotes the failure indicator. We write $\eta_{\max} = \max_j \eta_j$ for the vulnerability of the weakest judge. The \emph{panel attack success rate} is $\mathrm{ASR}(\mathcal{P}, a) = \Pr\!\bigl(\sum_{j=1}^K X_j^a \geq t\bigr)$.

To quantify diversity, we define a \emph{pairwise-MI diversity index} $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ from mutual information among judges:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:keff}
K_{\mathrm{eff}} = \frac{1}{K}\sum_{i=1}^{K} \Bigl[1 + \sum_{j \neq i} \exp\!\bigl(-2 \cdot \mathrm{MI}(i,j)\bigr)\Bigr].
\end{equation}
This formulation aggregates pairwise MI directly without matrix decomposition, and is more stable for small panels ($K{=}3$). It ranges from $K_{\mathrm{eff}} \approx K$ when judges are nearly independent ($\mathrm{MI} \to 0$) to $K_{\mathrm{eff}} \approx 1$ when judges are highly correlated ($\mathrm{MI} \to \infty$). Motivated by information-theoretic redundancy reduction, one can informally expect joint vulnerability to scale as $\eta_{\mathrm{joint}} \sim K_{\mathrm{eff}} \cdot \eta_{\max}$: independent channels multiply failure probabilities, so fewer effective channels (lower $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$) should reduce panel vulnerability. This motivating relationship assumes non-adaptive faults and independent channels; it serves as intuition rather than a formal bound.

\subsection{Adversary Model}

\begin{definition}[Adaptive Adversary]
\label{def:adversary}
A \emph{full-knowledge (oracle) adaptive adversary} has complete knowledge of the panel composition $\mathcal{P}$, the per-judge vulnerability profiles $\{\eta_j(\cdot)\}_{j=1}^K$, and the inter-judge correlation structure. It selects the attack $a^* = \arg\max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \mathrm{ASR}(\mathcal{P}, a)$ to maximize the panel ASR, yielding $\mathrm{ASR}^*(\mathcal{P}) = \sup_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \mathrm{ASR}(\mathcal{P}, a)$. This oracle model represents an \emph{upper bound} on adversarial capability; realistic adversaries with partial knowledge of panel composition would achieve lower ASR.
\end{definition}

This departs from BFT models~\cite{lamport1982byzantine,castro1999practical}, which tolerate $f < n/3$ faulty nodes but do not model adversaries that strategically target system structure. An adaptive adversary concentrates on the weakest link~\cite{schneier2000secrets}, suggesting a \emph{reversal}: under random faults, higher $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ reduces joint failure probability, but under adaptive adversaries, diverse judges may present a wider attack surface.

\paragraph{Weakest-Link Monotonicity.}
\label{thm:monotonicity}
Replacing any panel member with a pointwise more vulnerable judge can only increase $\mathrm{ASR}^*(\mathcal{P})$, for any adversary including adaptive ones (formal statement and proof in Appendix~\ref{app:proof}). This follows directly from the adversary model and is not a novel theoretical contribution. Stochastic dominance holds for 58\% of judge pairs (61/105), concentrating in cross-tier comparisons (79\% dominant) versus within-tier (28\%), predicting $\beta_2 > 0$ in Equation~\ref{eq:regression}. The relative importance of $\eta_{\max}$ versus $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ depends on $K$: at small $K$, one weak judge flips the majority; at larger $K$, the adversary must compromise multiple judges (\S\ref{sec:experiments}).

\section{Related Work}

\subsection{LLM-as-Judge and Multi-Judge Evaluation}

LLM judges gained traction after \citet{zheng2023judging} established them as scalable alternatives to human annotation. \citet{wang2023fair} documented systematic failure modes (position bias, verbosity, sycophancy) and \citet{ye2024justice} provided a comprehensive bias taxonomy, motivating multi-judge panels, while \citet{raina2024judgerobust} and \citet{maloyan2025investigating} quantified adversarial vulnerabilities including prompt injection. Multi-judge aggregation extends the classical noisy annotator framework \citep{dawid1979maximum}: \citet{verga2024replacing} showed diverse panels can match single large judges, and \citet{panickssery2024llm} identified self-preference bias underlying within-family correlations. Most relevant, \citet{almasoud2026security} reported that 7-model committees reduce ASR from 50--68\% to 10--19\% but did not separate diversity from individual robustness---the gap we address. Concurrent work benchmarks judge quality \citep{tan2024judgebench,li2024autoj} and judge--human agreement across tasks \citep{bavaresco2025llms}, adversarial risks in multi-agent systems \citep{tamas2025benchmarking}, and collaborative evaluation under benign conditions \citep{qian2026collabeval}; none tests whether aggregation benefits survive adversarial manipulation.

\subsection{Fault Tolerance, Voting Theory, and Ensemble Diversity}

The Condorcet Jury Theorem \citep{condorcet1785essai} proves that majority voting accuracy converges to 1 when per-voter accuracy exceeds 0.5; \citet{ladha1992condorcet} extended this to correlated voters, showing that positive correlation weakens the convergence guarantee. The CJT explicitly assumes independent, non-adversarial errors; its failure under adaptive adversaries is trivially expected \citep{tramer2018ensemble,kariyappa2019improving,pang2019improving}. Classical Byzantine fault tolerance \citep{lamport1982byzantine,castro1999practical,blanchard2017byzantine} similarly tolerates faulty nodes but assumes non-strategic faults; \citet{schneier2000secrets} articulated the weakest-link principle. Our framework adopts the Stackelberg structure of \citet{tambe2011security}. Correlated LLM judge errors \citep{kim2025correlated,kuai2026independent} violate both CJT and BFT independence assumptions; \citet{kuai2026independent} propose the Difficulty-Weighted Behavioral Entanglement Index (DBEI), weighting agreement by item difficulty, while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ captures a complementary dimension via pairwise MI. We focus on the \emph{panel-size-dependent structure} of this vulnerability: the weakest link's reduced predictive relevance at larger $K$, and the persistent positive association between diversity and vulnerability. Mitigation strategies include DBEI-based reweighting, Bayesian calibration, and credibility scoring \citep{ebrahimi2025adversary}.

\citet{krogh1995neural} formalized the bias-variance-covariance decomposition showing that ensemble error decreases with member diversity, but \citet{brown2005managing} demonstrated that diversity is beneficial only when individual members maintain sufficiently low bias---a condition violated when LLM panels include weak judges to maximize family coverage. \citet{kuncheva2003measures} showed that no pairwise diversity measure reliably predicts ensemble improvement, a finding we replicate: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\kappa$-diversity yield opposite $K$-dependent trajectories. \citet{lakshminarayanan2017simple} showed deep ensembles improve uncertainty calibration, but their analysis assumes i.i.d.\ noise rather than adversarial manipulation. Our positive $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$--ASR association under adversarial conditions---the reverse of the classical diversity benefit---instantiates Brown et al.'s caveat in the LLM judge setting: diversity co-occurs with capability reduction, and the capability cost dominates. To be explicit: the \emph{direction} of this association is predicted by classical theory; our incremental contributions are the position-bias exception (diversity \emph{reduces} vulnerability for shared-bias attacks), the operationalization dependence of the $K$-dependent trajectory, and the persistent association under adaptive adversaries---none of which follow from classical ensemble theory. Recent frameworks \citep{yang2026diversity,turkmen2026ensemble} characterize diversity under benign evaluation; we provide the adversarial complement.

\subsection{Adversarial Robustness of Evaluation Systems}

Adversarial vulnerability was formalized by \citet{goodfellow2015adversarial}, with transferability demonstrated by \citet{papernot2016transferability,papernot2017practical}. \citet{horvath2022boosting} showed that ensemble variance reduction improves certified robustness via randomized smoothing, but their framework assumes coordinated training with shared architectures---a control absent in post-hoc LLM judge selection. In NLP, \citet{wallace2019universal} introduced universal adversarial triggers and \citet{zou2023universal} showed gradient-based suffixes transfer across aligned LLMs. Controlled diversity via uncorrelated gradients \citep{kariyappa2019improving}, diversity regularization \citep{pang2019improving}, or vulnerability diversification \citep{zhu2020dverge} strengthens ensemble robustness in vision but requires coordinating member training. Our combinatorial design with 15 judges induces correlated residuals; \citet{mackinnon2018wild} developed the wild bootstrap for few-cluster settings, which we adapt with max-$p$ correction for the 3-position clustering structure (\S\ref{sec:robustness_method}). Our setting differs from both vision ensembles and NLP attacks: adversaries use natural-language manipulation rather than gradient perturbations, aggregation rules are a controllable design parameter, and we explicitly model adversarial manipulation interacting with multi-judge composition.

